---

```
KEY POINTS:
- inheritance = child class reuses parent's code
- class Dog(Animal) = Dog inherits from Animal
- child gets everything from parent automatically
- override = child replaces parent's method with own version
- Python checks child first, then parent if not found
- info() not overridden = all children use parent's version
- polymorphism = same method name, different behaviour per object
- loop through different objects, call same method = polymorphism
- Django APIView = parent class, your view = child class
- def get(self, request) in your view = overriding parent's get()
```

---

```python
# QUICK REFERENCE CODE

class Animal:
    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    def sound(self):
        print("Some generic animal sound")

    def info(self):
        print(f"{self.name} is an animal")  # inherited by all children


class Dog(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print(f"{self.name} says Woof!")  # overrides parent


class Cat(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print(f"{self.name} says Meow!")  # overrides parent


class Bird(Animal):
    def sound(self):
        print(f"{self.name} says Tweet!")  # overrides parent


class Fish(Animal):
    pass  # no override — uses parent's sound()


d1 = Dog("Rex")
c1 = Cat("Whiskers")
f1 = Fish("Nemo")

d1.sound()   # Rex says Woof!
c1.sound()   # Whiskers says Meow!
f1.sound()   # Some generic animal sound — falls back to parent
d1.info()    # Rex is an animal — inherited from parent

# polymorphism
animals = [Dog("Rex"), Cat("Whiskers"), Bird("Kiwi")]
for animal in animals:
    animal.sound()  # each handles differently
```

